guide.academic_exams.title: Academic Exams
guide.admission.p1: Universities do not admit "in general" — they admit onto routes. A category is one such route, say a grant competition or a paid track; inside it sit requirement profiles, the score variants of that route; and a funding option completes the picture. Category plus profile plus funding is called a choice, and everything on this site — chances, costs, tags — is computed per choice, which is why one university can look excellent on one route and closed on another.
guide.admission.p2: Requirements are minimums: clear them all and the choice considers you, miss one and it stays shut no matter how strong the rest of the profile is. But minimums only open the door — the average scores of admitted students show where the competition actually stands, so plan against the average and treat the minimum as the floor rather than the goal.
guide.admission.p3: Language proof follows the same logic with one extra switch. Mode any means a single proof from the list is enough; mode all means every listed proof is required. A native language counts wherever the route accepts it, and a single language rule can freely mix CEFR levels with exam scores.
guide.admission.p4: Scores from different countries are first brought to common scales — GPA, for instance, is normalized to 4.0 before anything is computed. Higher is better for every exam with a single exception, JLPT, where the level number runs the other way and a lower number means stronger Japanese.
guide.admission.title: How admission choices work
guide.exam_basics.p1: Every admission choice runs two independent checks: your academic scores and your language proof. The two never compensate for each other — a brilliant math score will not cover a missing IELTS — so both columns have to turn green for the choice to open.
guide.exam_basics.p2: Thresholds are set per university, per category, and per profile, which is why two tracks at the same university can demand completely different scores. Read the selected choice itself; the overview figures on a university page are orientation, not requirements.
guide.exam_basics.p3: The practical order never changes: close every minimum first, since a single failed minimum shuts the choice regardless of the rest, and only then push past the admitted average to turn a borderline application into a comfortable one.
