guide.ml.p1: Interests is a free-text query, not a set of tags. The system converts your text and the text of every university into vectors with the multilingual-e5 model and compares their cosine similarity, rescaled into a 0–1 score:
guide.ml.formula: sim = cosine(queryVec, uniVec)\nmlScore = clip((sim + offset - 0.55) / 0.35, 0, 1)\noffset = 0.038 for non-English queries, else 0
guide.ml.p2: That similarity becomes the semantic third of the {fit} score, which is why universities described the way you think float upward. Each university is represented by its name, programs, tags, description, and admission labels, and everyday abbreviations are expanded before matching — gamedev counts as game development, ML as machine learning — so you can write the way you speak.
guide.ml.p3: The query works best as short terms separated by commas: computer science, robotics, game development. Specificity pays off — "game development" outranks a vague "IT" — while every extra filler word dilutes the signal.
guide.ml.p4: Full sentences add noise, and negations fail completely: "no biology" still matches biology, because the model has no notion of exclusion. Write what you want, never what you want to avoid.
guide.ml.p5: Interests only reorder the list; they never override requirements or chances. And if the model itself is unavailable, interests change nothing at all: there is no quiet keyword fallback, the score is zero, and the page says so openly.
