guide.tags.title: Tags
guide.tags.intro: Tags compress the verdict onto the card so that a dozen universities can be scanned without opening each one. Standard tags state published facts, while AI tags interpret your profile — and when a tag is absent, its condition simply did not trigger.
guide.tags.standard_title: Standard Tags
guide.tags.standard.acceptance: Acceptance Rate carries the official selectivity figure wherever the university publishes one, so you can feel the competition before doing any math.
guide.tags.standard.aid_available: Aid Available means the university runs at least one grant track that is worth opening.
guide.tags.standard.over_budget: Over Budget warns that the estimated year cost runs above the budget in your profile — the option stays visible, but the gap is explicit.
guide.tags.standard.over_budget_aid: Over Budget • Aid Available is the same warning with a lifeline: the cost exceeds your budget, yet a grant track exists that could rewrite the math.
guide.tags.ai_note: AI tags appear only under {fit} sorting, because only that mode computes the numbers behind them.
guide.tags.ai_title: AI Tags
guide.tags.ai.conditional: Conditional / Exam Needed flags a choice where a required exam still has no evidence in your profile: the door is open, but a document is missing.
guide.tags.ai.your_vibe: Your Vibe marks near-perfect preference alignment — a mismatch of 0.14 or less across the four sliders.
guide.tags.ai.top_match: Good Match is its looser sibling: a mismatch of 0.22 or less, a solid fit that stops short of Your Vibe.
guide.tags.ai.likely_grant: Likely Grant highlights a grant route where your estimated chance reaches 65 or more.
guide.tags.ai.paid_admission: Paid Admission does the same job for the paid route at a general chance of 45 or more.
guide.tags.ai.requirements_met: Requirements Met confirms that every minimum of the active choice passes on your current data.
guide.tags.ai.below_requirements: Below Requirements is its opposite: at least one minimum of the active choice fails, and the chance section names the culprit.
