guide.roi.title: ROI (return on investment)
guide.roi.p1: ROI answers one narrow question: how does the money coming back compare to the money going in. It divides the estimated early-career salary of graduates by the cost of one study year, so a value of 2 means a single year of salary roughly repays two years of study:
guide.roi.formula: roi = salary / yearCost\nyearCost = cheapest track total, USD-normalized\nsalary = your major if matched, else average across majors\n> 2.0 Excellent Return, > 1.0 Positive Return, else High Investment
guide.roi.p2: The cost is the cheapest admission choice total for your study mode, converted into one currency so that universities from different countries stay comparable. The salary comes only from verified graduate data: your selected major when the university publishes figures for it, otherwise the average across its published majors.
guide.roi.p3: When a university publishes no usable salary data, the ROI block simply disappears instead of guessing. Missing data is never plotted as zero, because a zero would pretend the return is known to be terrible.
guide.roi.p4: Treat the number as a planning ratio rather than a promise: your program, country, skills, and right to work shape the real outcome far more than any average, and the labels follow fixed cutoffs — above 2.0 Excellent Return, above 1.0 Positive Return, anything below High Investment.
guide.roi.p5: Since ROI says nothing about whether you will get in at all, always read it next to {chance} and the full yearly cost: a brilliant ratio at an unreachable university is trivia, while a modest ratio at an affordable safety can be the smarter move.
guide.comparison.title: University comparison
guide.comparison.p1: Comparing lives on the Universities page in its own Comparing tab, while the rest of the catalog keeps working as usual — only this tab collects a shortlist. Pick universities from cards or map pins and press Compare, and click a picked card again to remove it, so putting two or three candidates side by side takes seconds.
guide.comparison.p2: The results table only ever compares like with like: the same academic year, the same fee status, the same scope. Each row then ends with an honest verdict — an advantage for one side, a trade-off, parity, incomparable data, or simply missing data — and the page never crowns an overall winner, because the best university depends on what you personally value.
guide.comparison.p3: Published selectivity is baked into the table, but it is not your admission probability: {chance} is computed separately and shown alongside. Anything the universities never published officially stays marked unavailable instead of quietly turning into a zero or a victory, so before any final decision, follow the table back to the official pages it cites.
guide.unichance.p1: {chance} estimates your admission probability from 0 to 100 for a single admission choice — a category plus a profile plus funding — never for a university as a whole. The number on the card belongs to your strongest choice inside that university, while the details page lays out every choice separately, so you can see exactly which route gives you the best odds. It is computed in layers:
guide.unichance.formula: z = (you - median) / std\nbase = Phi(z) * acceptFactor\nchance = base * context * gate + grantBoost\ncontext = clamp(0.55 + 0.25 * language + 0.20 * money, 0.35, 1.0)\ngate = clamp(1 - 0.78 * failShare, 0.18, 1.0)\nrange: high ±8, medium ±10, low ±14
guide.unichance.p2: At the core sits your score placed on the distribution of admitted students: the p25, median, and p75 percentiles fix a bell curve, your normalized score lands somewhere on it, and the area under the curve becomes the base probability. That base is then pulled back to earth — by the route's acceptance rate, thin language proof, a yearly cost above your budget, failed minimums — and lifted slightly for strong grant tracks.
guide.unichance.p3: Read the result in bands: 80–100 is high, 60–79 good, 40–59 medium, below 40 low. Every estimate also carries an uncertainty range that widens as the data thins out: ±8 points on verified score profiles, ±10 on medium confidence, ±14 on rough estimates.
guide.unichance.p4: Where there is nothing solid to compute from — no admitted-score data, missing exam evidence, a failed mandatory minimum — there is no number at all, only the reason: missing evidence, requirement not met, or no data. A missing estimate is never drawn as zero, because zero would pretend a certainty that nobody has.
guide.unichance.title: {chance} (estimated admission chance 0‑100)
guide.unifit.p1: {fit} is a sorting mode, not a filter. It ranks the whole catalog for your profile with a single number, and the smaller the number, the higher the university lands. That number blends three ingredients — preference mismatch, admission risk, and interest relevance — as a weighted sum:
guide.unifit.formula: mismatch = (|practice - uni| + |social - uni| + |city - uni| + |budget - uni|) / 4\nrisk = 1 - chance / 100\nsemantic = 1 - mlScore\nwith interests: score = 0.35 * mismatch + 0.30 * risk + 0.35 * semantic\nwithout interests: score = 0.60 * mismatch + 0.40 * risk
guide.unifit.p2: Change anything in the profile — an exam score, the budget, a slider — and the list is recomputed from scratch, so what you see always reflects your current data. Each ingredient is scaled from 0 to 1: mismatch is the mean distance between your four sliders and the university's four numbers, risk is one minus the {chance} probability, and semantic closeness is one minus the interests score. Without interests only the first two count, with heavier weights.
guide.unifit.p3: Your side of the comparison is four sliders — Focus, Atmosphere, Finance, Location — running from practice to science, social to hardcore, budget to prestige, city to outside. The Finance slider does double duty: besides measuring taste, it decides which {chance} feeds the risk — grant, paid, or their average in the middle. And because each admission choice is scored on its own, your strongest route can hide inside a university that sits lower in the overall list.
guide.unifit.p4: The badges on the cards are fixed cutoffs on these same numbers rather than a separate opinion. Your Vibe means a mismatch of 0.14 or less, Good Match 0.22 or less, Likely Grant a grant chance of 65 or more, and Paid Admission a general chance of 45 or more.
guide.unifit.p5: Missing exams never delete a university from the list. They show up on the chance side instead — as a conditional mark or as missing data — so you always see what is absent and what to add to sharpen the ranking.
